&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About This Role&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As Lead Nuclear Criticality Safety Engineer at General Matter, you&#39;ll build and lead the criticality safety program for a Part 70 fuel cycle facility from the ground up. Your criticality safety evaluations (CSEs) are a foundational input into our Integrated Safety Analysis (ISA), meaning the double-contingency approach, methodology, and technical judgment you establish now will shape how this facility operates for decades. This is a rare opportunity to set the criticality safety strategy for a next-generation enrichment facility rather than inherit one — and to do it at a company where your technical decisions have a direct line to whether we get licensed and operating on schedule.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&#39;re especially looking for engineers who&#39;ve done this before at an NRC-licensed Part 70 facility, or who bring deep DOE criticality safety experience and are ready to translate that rigor into the NRC licensing framework. If you&#39;ve spent your career fitting criticality safety work into someone else&#39;s program and are ready to build the program instead, we encourage you to apply and lead this work at General Matter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Responsibilities&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own the criticality safety program end-to-end: set methodology, double-contingency strategy, and technical direction for the site.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Develop and maintain nuclear criticality safety evaluations (CSEs) for processes involving fissile materials, and ensure CSE outputs are correctly integrated into the ISA as a foundational safety basis input.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Perform criticality safety analyses using computational methods and tools (e.g., SCALE, MCNP) to assess system safety and regulatory compliance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Serve as the primary technical point of contact on criticality safety matters during NRC licensing interactions, license amendments, and inspections.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ensure compliance with NRC, DOE, and ANSI/ANS criticality safety standards and regulations, translating DOE-derived methodologies into the NRC regulatory framework where applicable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mentor and provide technical review for other criticality safety engineers, setting the technical bar for the team as it grows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Conduct audits, inspections, and reviews of facility operations to identify and mitigate criticality safety risks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Collaborate with engineering, operations, and regulatory teams to implement effective safety measures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provide technical guidance and training to personnel on criticality safety principles and procedures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Investigate criticality safety incidents and develop corrective actions as necessary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Maintain accurate documentation and records to support audits and regulatory reviews.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Basic Qualifications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evidence of exceptional ability (prior projects, portfolio of work, completed products, etc.).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;8+ years of nuclear criticality safety experience, including direct authorship of criticality safety evaluations at a DOE or NRC-licensed facility.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bachelor&#39;s degree in Nuclear Engineering, Physics, or a related field.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong knowledge of criticality safety regulations, standards, and methodologies (e.g., 10 CFR Part 70, ANSI/ANS-8 series), and the ability to bridge DOE and NRC frameworks where they differ.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Proficiency in computational tools such as SCALE, MCNP, or similar software.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ability to perform trade studies and make clear recommendations using first principles and engineering fundamentals, even with partial information.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to convey technical information to regulators, operators, and leadership alike.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ability to obtain and maintain required security clearance and access authorization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Preferred Skills and Experience&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Direct experience standing up or significantly revising a criticality safety program at an NRC-licensed Part 70 facility.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;DOE weapons complex or national laboratory experience (e.g., Y-12, Savannah River, Los Alamos, Pantex, Idaho National Laboratory) with demonstrated ability to translate that experience into an NRC licensing context.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Certification as a Nuclear Criticality Safety Engineer (e.g., ANSI/ANS-8.24).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Professional Engineer (PE) license, or ability to obtain one.&lt;/li&gt;
